Below is a ready‑to‑paste HTML snippet that

1. **Creates a Google Map** centered on the library (34.052235, ‑118.243683).  
2. **Adds a marker** titled **“State Library”** at that same point.  
3. **Draws three circles** (each with a 100 m radius – you can change the radius if you wish) at the three coordinates you supplied.  

> **Important:** Replace `YOUR_API_KEY_HERE` with a valid Google Maps JavaScript API key that has the *Maps JavaScript API* enabled.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>State Library & City Hall Map</title>
  <style>
    /* Simple styling – make the map fill its container */
    #map {
      height: 400px;          /* same as the 600x400 size you asked for */
      width: 600px;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<h2>State Library – Map with Circles</h2>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // ---- 1️⃣  Load the Google Maps JavaScript API ----
  function initMap() {
    // ---- 2️⃣  Map options (center on the library) ----
    const map = new google.maps.Map(document.getElementById('map'), {
      center: { lat: 34.052235, lng: -118.243683 },
      zoom: 15,
      mapTypeId: 'roadmap'
    });

    // ---- 3️⃣  Add the “State Library” marker ----
    new google.maps.Marker({
      position: { lat: 34.052235, lng: -118.243683 },
      map,
      title: 'State Library'
    });

    // ---- 4️⃣  Helper to draw a circle ----
    function addCircle(lat, lng, radius = 100) {   // radius in metres
      new google.maps.Circle({
        strokeColor: '#FF0000',
        strokeOpacity: 0.6,
        strokeWeight: 2,
        fillColor: '#FF0000',
        fillOpacity: 0.2,
        map,
        center: { lat, lng },
        radius
      });
    }

    // ---- 5️⃣  Add the three circles you requested ----
    addCircle(34.052235, -118.243683);          // first coordinate
    addCircle(34.053690, -118.2423732);         // second coordinate
    addCircle(34.05381,  -118.242751);          // third coordinate
  }

  // ---- 6️⃣  Asynchronously load the Maps script with a callback ----
  const script = document.createElement('script');
  script.src = `https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/js?key=YOUR_API_KEY_HERE&callback=initMap`;
  script.defer = true;
  script.async = true;
  document.head.appendChild(script);
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How it works
| Step | What it does |
|------|--------------|
| **1** | Loads the Google Maps JavaScript API. |
| **2** | Creates a map centered on the library (the same coordinates you gave). |
| **3** | Places a marker titled **“State Library”** at that point. |
| **4‑5** | Draws three semi‑transparent red circles (default radius = 100 m) at the three latitude/longitude pairs you supplied. Adjust the `radius` argument in `addCircle()` if you need a larger or smaller circle. |
| **6** | Starts the map once the API script finishes loading. |

You can embed this snippet directly into any web page (e.g., inside a `<section>` of your site). If you prefer a static image instead of an interactive map, let me know and I can generate a static‑map URL with markers for you.

